Q: When would you choose Kafka over synchronous REST?

Main difference:
REST  → synchronous request/response (caller waits)
Kafka → asynchronous event/message communication (publish & continue)

Simple rule:
Need immediate response now? → REST
Need to publish work/event for later independent processing? → Kafka

Often use BOTH:
Client → REST → Order Service → Kafka → Payment / Email / Analytics

────────────────────────────────────────
When I would choose Kafka

1) No immediate response needed from downstream
OrderCreated event → Payment, Notification, Analytics consume independently.
Order API can return “accepted/created” without waiting for email/analytics.

2) Multiple consumers need the same event (fan-out)
REST: Order calls Payment + Email + Analytics (tight, brittle).
Kafka: each service subscribes with its own consumer group.

3) Loose coupling / temporal decoupling
A does not need B up at the exact same second.
Event stays in Kafka (within retention) until B consumes.
(Still need Kafka available; coupling moves to the broker/contract.)

4) Bursty traffic / buffering
Produce 10k/sec, consume 3k/sec → Kafka absorbs backlog (lag), consumers catch up.

5) Replay / new consumers
New analytics service can read historical events within retention — hard with plain REST.

────────────────────────────────────────
When I would NOT choose Kafka

Immediate request/response:
- GET customer
- login/auth
- balance check
- simple CRUD where caller needs confirmation now

Kafka request-reply is possible but usually overcomplicates these flows.

────────────────────────────────────────
REST vs Kafka

| Requirement               | REST | Kafka |
| ------------------------- | ---- | ----- |
| Immediate response        | ✅    | ❌*    |
| Synchronous communication | ✅    | ❌     |
| Asynchronous processing   | ⚠️   | ✅     |
| Loose coupling            | ⚠️   | ✅     |
| Multiple consumers        | ⚠️   | ✅     |
| Traffic buffering         | ❌    | ✅     |
| Event replay              | ❌    | ✅     |
| Simple CRUD               | ✅    | ❌     |
| Event-driven architecture | ⚠️   | ✅     |

*Kafka producer can block on ack, but that is broker durability ack — not “Payment finished.”

────────────────────────────────────────
Extra (good for 3–5 YOE)

- Don’t dual-write blindly (DB + Kafka) without outbox/CDC — risk of inconsistency.
- Prefer clear event contracts + schema registry for evolving payloads.
- “Order created” via REST to client; “OrderCreated” via Kafka to the ecosystem.

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answer

“I choose Kafka when work can be async and I want loose coupling, fan-out to many consumers, buffering under burst traffic, or replay.
Example: after creating an order via REST, publish OrderCreated and let payment, notification, and analytics consume independently.
I choose REST when the caller needs an immediate answer — get customer, login, validate. In real systems I use both: REST at the edge,
Kafka for downstream events.”
